 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Your bill may be large, but Perth's electricity price rises are among the smallest in the country.**\n\n**Electricity prices, annual change to August 2026 (ABS CPI)**\n- Perth: **+2.4%**. The index went from 100.0 in July 2025 to 102.44 from July 2026.\n- Australia (eight-capital average): **+13.2%**, after peaks of 37% in February 2026.\n- Perth's rise has been about 2.4–2.5% a year in the stable months (April–June 2025, April–June 2026 and August 2026). That fits WA's regulated household tariff, which moves in small annual steps.\n\n**Why the Perth figures look odd**\nThe Perth series swings wildly, for example −50% in August 2024 and +289% in August 2025. I think that's because government energy credits are counted in the CPI as price cuts. When they come and go, the index jumps. Those jumps aren't real changes in the rate you pay per unit. The index also doesn't show your bill, which depends on how much power you use.\n\n**Context**\n- Perth's overall CPI inflation is **4.3%** (August 2026), a bit above the national 4.0%. Your power bill is probably not the main source of the pressure.\n- Rebates ending would have added to your bill in the periods when they stopped. In the data, the effective price index was 69.2 in November–December 2025 and 100 by February 2026.\n\n**What else could be driving your bill**\n- Heavy air-conditioning or heating use, a pool, or an electric hot-water system.\n- A higher usage period, such as a bill that covers a hot stretch.\n- Whether your solar feed-in credits have fallen.\n- A bill that is larger because credits have dropped off, rather than because the tariff went up.\n\nIf you tell me what your bill was before and what it is now, and whether it's a bimonthly bill, I can help work out whether it's usage or price.",
